The error means that one side of the comparison is a Number and the other side is an Error/invalid field.

Your formula:

LookUp(
    colMethodComments,
    ID = ThisItem.ID
).MethodUserComment

The most likely cause is that ID does not exist in colMethodComments, or it is being interpreted differently than the SharePoint ID in ThisItem.ID.

Check the structure of colMethodComments

Add a label and set:

First(colMethodComments)

or inspect the collection in Collections view. Verify whether it contains:

ID
MethodUserComment

Common Fix #1: Use the correct key field

If your collection was built from SE Question List, it may contain QuestionNo instead of ID.

For example:

LookUp(
    colMethodComments,
    QuestionNo = ThisItem.QuestionNo
).MethodUserComment

Common Fix #2: Explicit numeric conversion

If the collection stores ID as text:

LookUp(
    colMethodComments,
    Value(ID) = ThisItem.ID
).MethodUserComment

or

LookUp(
    colMethodComments,
    ID = Value(ThisItem.ID)
).MethodUserComment

depending on which side is text.

Common Fix #3: The collection doesn't have an ID column

If you created colMethodComments using AddColumns() or ClearCollect(), the original SharePoint ID may not have been included.

For example, if your collection looks like:

ClearCollect(
    colMethodComments,
    AddColumns(
        Filter(...),
        MethodUserComment,
        ""
    )
)

then use whatever unique field exists, such as:

LookUp(
    colMethodComments,
    QuestionNo = ThisItem.QuestionNo
).MethodUserComment

To identify the exact issue

Show me the code where you create colMethodComments (the ClearCollect() or Collect() statement), and I can tell you exactly which field should be used in the LookUp().


